---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

/* The text here is the policy that was served from the app server until this
   page replaced it, word for word, at the same URL — the one in the app binary
   and in App Store Connect. Change the date at the top whenever the text
   changes; the policy itself says that is how a change is announced. */
const lastUpdated = '16 September 2026';
---

<Base
  title="Quiet Poker — Privacy Policy"
  description="What Quiet Poker holds about you, what it does not, and what stays on your iPhone."
  prose
>
  <h1>Quiet Poker &mdash; Privacy Policy</h1>
  <p class="updated">Last updated {lastUpdated}</p>

  <p>
    Quiet Poker is a play-money poker practice app. The chips in it have no cash
    value, cannot be cashed out, and cannot be wagered. This policy describes
    what the app and its server hold about you, and what they do not.
  </p>

  <h2>What we do not do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      We do not track you, and we do not share anything with advertisers or data
      brokers. The app contains no advertising or analytics software from
      anyone.
    </li>
    <li>
      We do not ask for your location, contacts, photos, microphone, camera, or
      health data, and the app cannot read them.
    </li>
    <li>
      We never receive your card or payment details. Apple handles payment and
      tells us only that a purchase happened.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What we hold</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Your account.</strong> If you sign in with Apple, we store the
    identifier Apple gives us for you, and your email address if you chose to
    share it rather than hide it. This is what lets your chips and your
    subscription follow you to another device. You can play offline without
    signing in, and then we hold nothing about you at all.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Purchases and subscription.</strong> When you buy chips or subscribe,
    we store the App Store transaction identifier, which product it was, whether
    it came from the sandbox or production, when it happened, and how many play
    chips it credited. We keep the state of your subscription and when its paid
    period ends. We need these to give you what you paid for and to make sure a
    purchase is never credited twice.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Your chips.</strong> We keep your play-chip balance and a record of
    the transfers that produced it &mdash; purchases, daily rewards, and the
    results of hands you played online.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Online games.</strong> While you play in a private room we store the
    room, who is seated in it, and the results of the hands, so a disconnection
    does not cost you your stack. Other players see the name and the avatar you
    chose, and nothing else about you.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Your device.</strong> We use Apple&rsquo;s App Attest to confirm that
    results sent from a device really came from an unmodified copy of this app.
    It gives us a key for that device, not an identifier that follows you
    around.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Server logs.</strong> Our server keeps short-lived operational logs
    to diagnose failures. Room diagnostics are written with player cards and
    identifiers removed.
  </p>

  <h2>What stays on your iPhone</h2>
  <p>
    Hands you play offline, your hand history, your statistics, and your chosen
    card back and table colour stay on your device. They are not uploaded.
  </p>

  <h2>Who else sees it</h2>
  <p>
    Apple, because sign-in and payment go through them. Our own hosting
    provider, because the server runs on their machine. Nobody else. We do not
    sell anything about you to anyone.
  </p>

  <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
  <p>
    We keep your account and its chip ledger while your account exists. Purchase
    records are kept as long as required for tax and dispute handling.
    Operational logs are short-lived.
  </p>
  <p>
    When you delete your account, what identifies you goes: the Apple
    identifier, your email, and every session. Signing in with Apple afterwards
    starts a new account rather than reopening the old one. The chip and
    purchase history stays, without you attached to it, because tax and dispute
    rules require those records to outlive the account.
  </p>

  <h2>Your choices</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Play without an account: offline play needs no sign-in.</li>
    <li>Hide your email when you sign in with Apple; we then never see it.</li>
    <li>
      Manage or cancel a subscription in the App Store, from Settings on your
      iPhone or from the Shop tab in the app.
    </li>
    <li>
      Delete your account and its chips yourself, from the Profile tab. It
      cannot be undone, and it is refused while chips of yours are still seated
      at a table or held by a private room you opened &mdash; stand up and close
      the room first. See the <a href="/support#delete-account">support page</a>.
    </li>
    <li>Ask for your data or its deletion using the contact address below.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>
    Quiet Poker is not directed at children, and we do not knowingly collect
    anything about them.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>If this policy changes, the date at the top changes with it.</p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>Write to <a href="mailto:support@quietpoker.app">support@quietpoker.app</a>.</p>
</Base>

<style>
  .updated {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }
</style>
